## Implementation Steps

### 1. Launching the EC2 Instance

An Amazon EC2 instance was launched using Amazon Linux as the operating system.

The instance was configured to act as a Linux-based cloud server for hosting the website.

### 2. Configuring the Security Group

A custom Security Group was configured with the following inbound rules:

* SSH - Port 22 - Restricted to my IP address
* HTTP - Port 80 - Allowed from Anywhere IPv4

This configuration allowed secure server administration through SSH and public access to the hosted website.

### 3. Connecting to the Server Using SSH

The EC2 instance was accessed securely from a local computer using SSH and an EC2 key pair.

The connection provided command-line access to the Amazon Linux server.

### 4. Installing Linux

The Linux web server was installed on the Amazon Linux EC2 instance using the command line.

The server was started and configured to start automatically when the EC2 instance boots.

### 5. Verifying the Web Server

The Linux service was checked using system commands to confirm that it was active and running.

The default Linux  webpage was successfully accessed through the EC2 instance's public IPv4 address.

### 6. Deploying the Custom Website

The default Linux webpage was replaced with a custom HTML webpage containing the message:

*Welcome to DecodeLabs*

The webpage also displays the project name, server status, and technologies used in the deployment.

### 7. Public Website Access

The final custom website was successfully accessed through the EC2 instance's public IPv4 address using a web browser.

---

## Security Configuration

The EC2 Security Group was configured with only the required inbound access:

| Service | Port | Access        |
| ------- | ---: | ------------- |
| SSH     |   22 | My IP         |
| HTTP    |   80 | Anywhere IPv4 |

This allowed secure administration access while making the website publicly accessible.

